From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Tue, 05 Jul 94 00:44:47 EDT
Subject: [#94-7-12] Nanotechnology and uncertainty 

Im an earlier post I discussed the possibility of an AI breakthrough causing
great uncertainty, Chris Hibbert provides a good plausible counterargument
to my post.  Robin Hanson concurs with Chris Hibbert.  I will try to show
the problems with their reasoning.

Chris says:
>What actually happens in the real world
>is that everyone moves forward by steps, and so in the year in which someone
>invents a 1000 brainpower machine, some of the other competitors will have
>invented 900 brainpower machines 3 months earlier, and others will come out 
>with 1500 brainpower machines 3 months later.  No one gets to be a factor of 
>1000 ahead of everyone else at any point.  Someone is always the first to 
>reach any particular milestone, but everyone else was close a little while 
>ago, or will do better in a little bit.

This expresses a general viewpoint about technological change that is
valuable and often accurate but I think it is incomplete and sometimes simply
inaccurate. Consider a general reformulation of Chris Hibbert's thesis:

Technological change is incremental. There is a continuity in the powers
which result from technological advancement.  When multiple groups are
working to develop a technology do not expect one group to achieve a
capability which is several orders of magnitude superior to the capabilities
of competing groups.  Instead, expect nearly simultaneous technological
advancement. Also, expect groups to leap-frog one another or at least stay
within a narrow band of capability.

If there is an environment of secrecy and war I do not think developments in
nanotechnology or AI will conform to the thesis above.  Technological
advancement will certainly remain incremental; however, increments can be
very large. Also, some groups will not keep up and instead fall further
behind.  Consider a technology whose development and deployment differed in
important ways from the thesis above, i.e., one of the most important modern
war technologies:  the nuclear bomb.

Consider first, the experiences of the US and Japan in World War II.

July 16, 1945 detonation of first atomic bomb by US
August 6, 1945 bombing of Hiroshima
August 9, 1945 bombing of Nagasaki
August 14, 1945 Japan surrenders

The first atomic bomb had a power of 21 kilotons.  This was 2000 times more
powerful than any bomb used during the entire war in Europe.  Japan did have
a small nuclear research program but it greatly lagged the US program and
was falling further behind.  When the war planners of Japan were confronted
with the destruction of Hiroshima and Nagaski they were faced with enormous
uncertainty. They did not have 90% of an atomic bomb, nor 50%. They had no
atomic bomb at all.  They might have wondered: How many bombs did the US
have? How powerful would other successor bombs be? If it took one plane to
deliver a bomb, might the US next destroy dozens of cities in a day?  In
fact, the US only had two bombs ready to attack Japan and they were both
used.  The atomic bomb was such a large advance and the uncertainty was
so great that Japan surrendered.

Consider next, the experiences of Germany.

Germany had a significant nuclear research program with top scientists like
Werner Heisenberg. Germany fell before the atomic bomb was used but
interesting insights into the German program were obtained by British
intelligence. When a group of German nuclear scientists (including
Heisenberg) were captured by the British they were detained together in a
facility with secret audio bugs. Conversations recorded by the bugs reveal
that the Germans were shocked by the news of the bombing of Hiroshima. Some
of the scientists reproached themselves for incompetence.  The tapes also
reveal that Heisenberg had incorrectly calculated the amount of matter
necessary to achieve critical mass. His significant overestimate made the
project seem hardly feasible. Heisenberg instead oriented the program
towards the development of a nuclear reactor for energy. (Information
about the tapes is from an article in the New York Review of Books from
several months ago.)

Consider the experiences of the former USSR.

After a delay of four years, in August 1949, the USSR detonated an atomic
bomb.  Their program was greatly aided by the espionage of Klaus Fuchs and
others. I consider four years to be a very long time relative to the
strategic value of the atomic bomb breakthrough.  If the US had been a
ruthless power bent on world domination it might have attempted to stop the
USSR program.  Of course, the USSR was an ally of the US in World War II and
the US did not use its nuclear monopoly to attempt to completely subjugate
the world.  Yet, one can imagine the first country to obtain a nuclear bomb
using it to threaten or to destroy the scientists and the infrastructure of
competing nuclear development programs. Israel apparently crippled and
delayed the nuclear program of Iraq when it used a conventional bombing
strike against a nuclear reactor in Iraq.  Stopping or delaying the program
of an opponent might be particularly effective because there is another
large jump in power which occurs when the Hydrogen bomb is developed.

Consider the development of the H-bomb.

After the construction of the atomic bomb there was no great push in the US
toward the development of the H-bomb although it was thought possible.
Edward Teller was an advocate and he did theoretical calculations. When the
USSR detonated its atomic bomb in 1949, the US began work in earnest on the
H-bomb.

November, 1952 detonation of the first H-Bomb by the US.

This bomb was 10.4 megatons, approximately 500 times bigger than the Nagasaki
atomic bomb.  We are now talking about bombs which are approximately one
million times more powerful than conventional devices. I believe a ruthless
country could have achieved a long-lived monopoly in both A-bombs and
H-bombs - an enormous advantage.  Instead, the US lost its A-bomb monopoly
in 1949 and the H-bomb monopoly in 1953 when the USSR detonated its bombs.

Conclusions: The development of nuclear bomb technology deviates from the
incrementalist model described at the beginning of this note.  It is
possible for one side to obtain a ``1,000 factor'' advantage.  In fact, it
appears one side could have obtained a ``1,000,000 factor'' advantage. The
technological advantage was maintained for 4 years and not just three months.
The opposing sides were not leap-frogging one another. The Japanese and
Germans appear to have been falling further behind.

Complaint 1: The advantage is overestimated by looking at the power of
individual bombs.  Initially, there were few atomic bombs. Also, consider
the Tokyo and Dresden firebombings which were done with conventional bombs
and by some measures were more destructive.

Reply: This complaint is really asking what metric(s) should be used to
measure advances. There are clearly many metrics and it is a simplification
to measure an advance by just one. Note, the Tokyo bombing required sorties
by hundreds of bombers.  The Hiroshima atomic bombing was done with one
bomber, the Enola Gay.  Nuclear radiation also has different effects on
death rates, etc. As for the limited number of bombs; well, that is part of
the uncertainty an opponent must face. Bomb production was ramped up after
feasibility was shown. I think most everyone will admit H-bombs are
massively more deadly than conventional bombs by a wide variety of metrics.

Complaint 2: Japan was just waiting for an excuse to surrender.

Reply 2: This is controversial. In any case, they did not. Nuclear bombs were
decisive in precipitating a surrender.

Complaint 3: AI and nanotechnology development will not be like nuclear
weapons development. It will follow the incrementalist model.

Reply 2: We do not know exactly what the development of these technologies
will entail.  The best pathway to nanotech is currently unknown. There are
plenty of excellent ideas.  But note the uncertainty, a group which follows
the wrong path may find itself at a dead-end or greatly delayed.
  I think algorithm development may be key to the creation and deployment of
both technologies and algorithm development often does not follow the
incrementalist model. See my other post.


