From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Tue, 05 Jul 94 10:30:46 EDT
Subject: [#94-7-13] Nanotechnology and uncertainty 

Suppose multiple competing groups are attempting to develop AI and/or
nanotech. Another post discussed how competing groups obtained widely
varying results when developing nuclear weapons. But what about AI and
nanotech?  How much divergence will there be?  
  The methods and techniques that will be used to develop AI and nanotech
are only currently known in rather sketchy broad outlines.  So, let's
consider a specific relevant area where experience is available, i.e.,
algorithm design. This will probably be a core facilitator technology.

Here is an example from the algorithm design area where the time complexity
of the solution of a problem was reduced from:

 (1)  a supercomputer taking 10^46 years to solve an instance to
 (2)  an Apple II computer solving an instance on stage during a conference.

One of the early public-key cryptographic systems was proposed by Merkle and
Hellman.  The system was based on a variant of the knapsack problem.  The
knapsack problem is known to be NP-complete; but, the variant used a
disguised superincreasing set of weights and was initially of unknown
difficulty. It was easy to show that messages that were encoded using the
Merkle-Hellman system could not be decoded in a reasonable period of time,
if a brute force search algorithm was used.  See (1) above.  Other competing
researchers, especially Shamir, showed how to improve on the performance of
the brute-force algorithm with innovative ideas. They demonstrated how to
reduce the time to decode a message from (1) to (2) above.  (See Applied
Cryptography by Schneier pg 277-281) 

Here is another example from the algorithm design area.

One of the results in my thesis was the first polynomial-time algorithm to
solve a problem in fault diagnosability. Previous algorithms in the
literature all had exponential time complexities. The speedup was quite large
in practice and grew with the size of the problem instance being solved.
(See Sullivan Symp. on Foundations of Computer Science FOCS 1984)

Many more examples.

Check out the proceedings of major computer conferences covering algorithm
design.  They record many algorithmic breakthroughs. It is not necessary to
go from exponential to polynomial time for a big win. Reductions in time
complexity from O(n^2), say, to O(n log(n)) can also lead to very large
savings in practice.  (It depends on the size of problem instances and
constants of proportionality.)  (Another shameless self-reference: Kosaraju
and Sullivan, Symposium on Theory of Comp. STOC 1988, contains the first
polynomial time algorithm for cycle detection in dynamic graphs.)

I am not saying that nanotech or AI can be reduced to specific problems with
exponential or polynomial time complexities or anything like that.  I am
saying that algorithm design will probably play a big part in progress in
both areas and further that results in algorithm design often yield
massive performance discontinuities.

Suppose competing teams have the ability to construct modules of the same raw
computational power, i.e., the same number of basic functional units and
approximately the same degree of communication interconnectivity.  In this
case, the precise organization of the functional units, the connectivity and
the programming become decisive.  A module might yield a ``brain'' with
intelligence roughly equivalent to a normal adult, a genius, a mentally
deficient person, a dolphin or something even stranger.

An open literature, cooperation, free exchange of people and information,
and economic integration are ideas which would reduce the discontinuities
in technical advances between nations.  Secretive, antagonistic development
would increase differences in advancement.  Could one side gain a massive
advantage? It seems quite possible to me - for good or ill.
                       gfs

