From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 5 Jul 1994 18:00:14 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-19] Nanotechnology and uncertainty 

Gregory Sullivan argues, using the development of algorithms and
nuclear weapons as examples, that sometimes one "side" can increase
the efficiency of some specific technology by orders of magnitude.
He argues that this is a risk we should take seriously, and that 

>An open literature, cooperation, free exchange of people and information,
>and economic integration are ideas which would reduce the discontinuities
>in technical advances between nations.  Secretive, antagonistic development
>would increase differences in advancement.  

I agree with all of this.  However, Greg has also said:

>I am leaning toward agreeing with Drexler that we should have international
>cooperation and tight multi-lateral international governmental controls on
>the development of nanotechnology.; even though I see many problems with
>a powerful `state'.

Unless you want to argue that the risk of sudden advancement is much
*more* likely for nanotech than for other technology, you must either
argue that *all* technology should be under "tight multi-lateral
international governmental controls", or that none should.  Showing
the possibiliby of sudden advancement for technologies in general
doesn't help distinguish nanotech from other technologies.

And even if you show it is more likely, you must still show it is more
likely enough.  The risk of a sudden nanotech jump without
international control, and the benefits such control would give in
this case, must be weighed against all the harm such controls could do
if there is no such sudden nanotech jump.

Robin Hanson

